1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,097
Five in the morning,

2
00:00:02,097 --> 00:00:04,770
the day after Thanksgiving.

3
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:07,150
A crowd has been waiting outside in

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:09,973
the cold for a television priced so

5
00:00:09,973 --> 00:00:12,370
low it looks like a typo.

6
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:14,055
The doors open,

7
00:00:14,055 --> 00:00:16,700
and the store starts losing money.

8
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:18,481
On purpose.

9
00:00:18,717 --> 00:00:19,474
And yet,

10
00:00:19,474 --> 00:00:20,232
last year,

11
00:00:20,232 --> 00:00:23,365
Americans spent eleven point eight billion

12
00:00:23,365 --> 00:00:26,212
dollars online on Black Friday alone,

13
00:00:26,212 --> 00:00:27,230
a record,

14
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:29,397
according to Adobe.

15
00:00:29,737 --> 00:00:31,937
Retailers plan their whole year around

16
00:00:31,937 --> 00:00:33,197
this weekend.

17
00:00:33,347 --> 00:00:35,030
So how does a day built on

18
00:00:35,030 --> 00:00:37,553
discounts actually make money?

19
00:00:37,817 --> 00:00:40,907
Let's follow the money.

20
00:00:41,227 --> 00:00:42,916
The deal that loses,

21
00:00:42,916 --> 00:00:44,605
the cable that wins,

22
00:00:44,605 --> 00:00:47,153
the quarter that pays for the year,

23
00:00:47,153 --> 00:00:50,902
and everyone who quietly takes a cut.

24
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:06,493
First,

25
00:01:06,493 --> 00:01:07,610
the name.

26
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:10,010
It has nothing to do with profit.

27
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:12,014
In nineteen sixty-one,

28
00:01:12,014 --> 00:01:15,082
a public relations newsletter reported

29
00:01:15,082 --> 00:01:17,894
that police officers in Philadelphia

30
00:01:17,894 --> 00:01:20,450
called the days after Thanksgiving Black

31
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:22,630
Friday and Black Saturday.

32
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:23,405
For them,

33
00:01:23,405 --> 00:01:24,982
it meant jammed streets,

34
00:01:24,982 --> 00:01:27,720
packed sidewalks and long shifts.

35
00:01:27,983 --> 00:01:31,183
Downtown merchants hated the name.

36
00:01:31,193 --> 00:01:33,302
According to History dot com,

37
00:01:33,302 --> 00:01:35,007
they tried to rebrand it as Big

38
00:01:35,007 --> 00:01:36,033
Friday.

39
00:01:36,033 --> 00:01:37,544
It didn't stick.

40
00:01:37,783 --> 00:01:38,218
Then,

41
00:01:38,218 --> 00:01:40,254
in the late nineteen-eighties,

42
00:01:40,254 --> 00:01:42,913
retailers found a happier story.

43
00:01:43,043 --> 00:01:45,102
This is the day stores finally move

44
00:01:45,102 --> 00:01:46,523
from red ink to black ink,

45
00:01:46,523 --> 00:01:48,153
from loss to profit.

46
00:01:48,273 --> 00:01:49,933
It's a great story.

47
00:01:50,103 --> 00:01:51,903
But it's a reinvention.

48
00:01:51,903 --> 00:01:54,009
The police came first.

49
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:54,789
Still,

50
00:01:54,789 --> 00:01:57,650
the myth points at a real question.

51
00:01:57,650 --> 00:01:59,462
How does a store make money on

52
00:01:59,462 --> 00:02:01,195
a day of discounts?

53
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:07,640
Start with the doorbuster.

54
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:09,874
It's a small batch of products priced

55
00:02:09,874 --> 00:02:11,612
so low that the store may earn

56
00:02:11,612 --> 00:02:13,800
little or nothing on them.

57
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:16,230
Sometimes less than nothing.

58
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:19,000
Economists call it a loss leader.

59
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,950
Its job isn't profit.

60
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:22,880
Its job is to get you through

61
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,031
the door.

62
00:02:24,283 --> 00:02:26,293
Because once you're inside,

63
00:02:26,293 --> 00:02:29,223
you rarely buy just one thing.

64
00:02:29,403 --> 00:02:31,463
You came for the television.

65
00:02:31,573 --> 00:02:33,296
You leave with a wall mount,

66
00:02:33,296 --> 00:02:34,270
a cable,

67
00:02:34,270 --> 00:02:35,244
a soundbar,

68
00:02:35,244 --> 00:02:36,993
and a cart full of things that

69
00:02:36,993 --> 00:02:39,341
were never the headline deal.

70
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:42,874
Then comes the most profitable question in

71
00:02:42,874 --> 00:02:44,023
retail.

72
00:02:44,263 --> 00:02:46,493
Would you like the protection plan?

73
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:49,819
Economists Jose Miguel Abito and Yuval

74
00:02:49,819 --> 00:02:52,407
Salant studied extended warranties,

75
00:02:52,407 --> 00:02:55,256
which averaged about twenty-four percent

76
00:02:55,256 --> 00:02:57,043
of a product's price.

77
00:02:57,243 --> 00:02:59,960
Using failure rates for televisions,

78
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,700
they estimated profit margins as high as

79
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:05,584
sixty-two to seventy-three percent.

80
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:07,910
Let me draw the trade.

81
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:09,666
Box one is the doorbuster,

82
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:10,977
with a thin margin,

83
00:03:10,977 --> 00:03:12,910
or a negative one.

84
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:15,114
Box two is everything else in the

85
00:03:15,114 --> 00:03:15,760
cart.

86
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:16,656
The add-ons,

87
00:03:16,656 --> 00:03:17,322
the plan,

88
00:03:17,322 --> 00:03:18,920
the full-price items.

89
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,430
The arrow between them is foot traffic.

90
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:23,385
The loss on box one is simply

91
00:03:23,385 --> 00:03:25,696
the price of a visitor.

92
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:27,894
Some deals are built for the day

93
00:03:27,894 --> 00:03:28,910
itself.

94
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:31,597
Consumer Reports has described low-priced

95
00:03:31,597 --> 00:03:34,261
TVs made specifically for Black Friday,

96
00:03:34,261 --> 00:03:37,100
often sold by a single retailer.

97
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,610
The industry calls them derivatives.

98
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:42,098
They're similar to regular models,

99
00:03:42,098 --> 00:03:44,783
but with different model numbers and often

100
00:03:44,783 --> 00:03:46,102
fewer features,

101
00:03:46,102 --> 00:03:48,045
like fewer HDMI inputs.

102
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:49,139
To be fair,

103
00:03:49,139 --> 00:03:51,553
one derivative that Consumer Reports

104
00:03:51,553 --> 00:03:54,186
tested cost about one hundred and seventy

105
00:03:54,186 --> 00:03:56,161
dollars less than the model it was

106
00:03:56,161 --> 00:03:57,017
based on,

107
00:03:57,017 --> 00:03:59,153
and performed just as well.

108
00:03:59,303 --> 00:04:01,353
The catch is comparison.

109
00:04:01,353 --> 00:04:03,358
If only one store sells that model

110
00:04:03,358 --> 00:04:04,004
number,

111
00:04:04,004 --> 00:04:06,455
you can't easily check the price anywhere

112
00:04:06,455 --> 00:04:07,277
else.

113
00:04:07,517 --> 00:04:09,597
And the store doesn't always pay for

114
00:04:09,597 --> 00:04:10,977
